From ca0042bc33b8c167fec3feec9e98bb909db4670e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: stephan-xyz <stephan@zehrer.xyz>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 14:51:43 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] dwc2d: USB serial adapters as the scheme usb-serial (CDC-ACM,
 FTDI, CP210x, CH340)

A CDC-ACM device (communications interface 2/2 with a data interface)
or a known FTDI (0403:6001/6010/6011/6014/6015), CP210x (10c4:ea60...)
or CH340/CH341 (1a86:7523/5523/7522) adapter, found at boot or plugged
in later, becomes /scheme/usb-serial/<ports>, named by its hub ports
from the root like USB sticks (e.g. 1.3). Zigbee and Thread coordinator
sticks (SONOFF ZBDongle, SkyConnect) and many Arduino boards are one of
these.

Read and write the port for bytes; reads block (EWOULDBLOCK until data
comes, EAGAIN with O_NONBLOCK) and raise read events. tcgetattr and
tcsetattr work as on a pty: speed, data bits, parity and stop bits come
from c_cflag. /scheme/usb-serial/<ports>/baud reads and sets the speed
as a number. Ports start at 115200 8N1; DTR and RTS go up on open.
Listing /scheme/usb-serial gives the ports; an unplugged adapter's
handles return ENODEV.

The open port is read on host channel 7 every 5 ms: a transfer that
stays armed when the adapter is on the root hub or a high-speed hub,
packet by packet through the transaction translator for a full-speed
adapter behind a high-speed hub (Pi 3B+). Writes go out on channel 0.
One port is read at a time. /tmp/dwc2d-serial-log makes dwc2d print
what goes through; "serial" in /tmp/dwc2d-cycle switches the adapter's
port off and on.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
